    "flags": {
        "cinderfall": {
            "tagline": "Every promise in Cinderfall is collateral of some kind. Yours just happens to swing a warhammer, and something is always watching to see if you default.",
            "role": "Bound Defender",
            "pillar": "Cuts across all three — the Oath, not the power source, defines you",
            "hpFormula": "10 + Con/level",
            "keyAbilityText": "Strength or Charisma (choose at 1st)",
            "terms": [
                [
                    "The Oath",
                    "The bond you take at 1st level — permanent, and the thing every other class feature hangs off of. Three known forms exist: the Binding Clause, the Cinder Vow, and the Unwritten Oath."
                ],
                [
                    "The Ward",
                    "Whoever or whatever your Oath obligates you to protect — a contract-holder, a marked target, a person, a place, an idea. Defined by your Oath's terms, not always by your preference."
                ],
                [
                    "The Answer",
                    "Your signature reaction. Something threatens the Ward; you Answer it, in person, immediately. What \"Answer\" means changes hard by Oath."
                ],
                [
                    "Conviction",
                    "Your Focus-equivalent resource, refilled on rest, spent on Vows. Called Leverage under a Binding Clause, Cinders under a Cinder Vow, and Resolve under an Unwritten Oath — same pool, different name, different flavor."
                ],
                [
                    "Vows",
                    "The Conviction-fueled support abilities you know, gained by level. Small, reliable, always-available — not spells so much as things you can absolutely be counted on to do."
                ],
                [
                    "The Trial &amp; Forsworn",
                    "The Trial is what happens when your Oath is genuinely tested (see §03). Forsworn is what happens if it breaks — a real status with real consequences, not a fluff demotion."
                ]
            ],
